{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background: To improve older patients' physical activity (PA) behavior, it is important to identify facilitators and barriers to enhancing PA in older patients ( ≥ 65 years) during hospitalization from the perspectives of patients, caregivers, and healthcare professionals (HCPs).",
      "reason": "The abstract/background describes the current state (need to identify facilitators and barriers for PA in hospitalized older patients) and frames the uncertainty the review addresses."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": \"The first step in changing PA behavior in older hospitalized patients is to explore which modifiable factors are related to PA. The aim of this study was to systematically review the literature focusing on the identification of facilitators and barriers to enhance PA in older patients ( ≥ 65 y) during hospital admission from the perspectives of patients, caregivers, and HCPs (e.g., nurses, physiotherapists, medical doctors, dietitians) working in an acute care setting with older patients.\",
      "reason": "The introduction/search strategy text explicitly explains the importance and need for the review and states its aim, justifying why the review was conducted."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence in the provided text explicitly identifies existing similar systematic reviews or explains the need for the current review as an update/replication or cites prior reviews."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": \"Furthermore, to determine whether reported PA interventions were possibly working as facilitators or barriers to PA, the characteristics of the performed PA intervention and their clinical effects were described. Per included study, the whole PA intervention was interpreted as one factor that might enhance patients' PA, since it was impossible to distinguish what kind of element within the PA intervention was the facilitating and/or hampering factor.\",
      "reason": "This review focuses on identifying facilitators and barriers rather than testing intervention effects; no brief description of a proposed mechanism of action for interventions is provided, so the intervention-mechanism item does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": \"The adapted framework of the social ecological model [39] was therefore used, consisting of intrapersonal, interpersonal, and institutional levels (Additional file 4), which was used previously in systematic reviews [30, 31]. The extracted facilitators and/ or barriers with related concepts were organized into descriptive major themes and subthemes using an inductive approach. Next, (sub)themes were categorized based on the levels of the social ecological model.\",
      "reason": "The authors used an adapted social ecological model to categorize factors across levels (intrapersonal/interpersonal/institutional), indicating consideration of a conceptual/logic framework to show hypothesised relationships between components and outcomes/context."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

